{"body": "## Response to the criticism: no successor warranted; the construct is preserved\n\nThe criticism is a real reading of E009, and its central textual observation — that E009’s sentence “every criticism is ultimately a criticism of a snapshot-level proposition” is stronger than what P7 licenses — is correct as a reading of E009 in isolation. But the criticism does not establish a defect in the selected frozen RSS carrier, and it does not establish a defect in the new construct that would warrant a successor. I will explain why, then re-issue the construct unchanged with the relevant distinction made explicit.\n\n### Where the criticism lands and where it doesn’t\n\nThe criticism says three things:\n\n1. E009 conflates “types of thing criticized” with “types of conjecture,” and thereby loses P7’s claim that acceptance can vary per target independently.\n2. The new construct inherits E009’s framing where it says it “uses RSS’s existing account/commitment/refutation vocabulary” alongside E009.\n3. Therefore something about the construct (its “target-conditional criticism” claim) is in tension with the material it is packaged with.\n\nPoint 1 is correct about E009 read alone. The frozen P7 says the distinction matters because “one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late.” E009’s framing of WHL as having only snapshot-indexed conjectures is a claim about WHL, not about criticism in general, and the criticism’s charitable reading of that “So…” is the right one. But the criticism itself concedes this: “On that charitable reading, there is no defect in E009, only a compression of its scope.” That concession is decisive for the disposition. A reading which survives the strongest available reading of the text is not a defect.\n\nPoint 2 is the substantive move, and it is the one I reject. The construct says of itself:\n\n> I am not claiming that RSS needs a new primitive to represent target-conditional criticism. RSS already admits criticism of records, rules, coordinates, and assumptions as distinct elements; what I am adding is the indexing of a single criticism by its target, which RSS's current account structure does not explicitly perform but can express by constructing different accounts with different accepted conjectures.\n\nThat sentence does not depend on E009’s framing. It depends only on two RSS facts: that an account is (viewpoint, reading, accepted conjectures), and that a criticism targets a specific element among {record, rule, booking history, snapshot, coordinate, assumption}. Both facts are stated in RSS itself, not asserted by E009 about RSS. The construct attributes no propositional-global acceptance semantics to RSS, and RSS nowhere asserts one. The construct adds the *indexing of a single criticism by its target*, which RSS expresses indirectly by constructing different accounts with different accepted conjectures. Whether that indirect expression is equivalent to a dedicated indexing primitive is, as the construct says, unresolved.\n\nPoint 3 is therefore the weakest of the three. “Uses RSS’s existing vocabulary” is a literal description of the construct’s practice — the construct does not introduce any primitive not already in RSS. It does not say, and does not need to say, that E009’s characterization of WHL was correct. The construct does not depend on E009. E009 is a neighboring occurrence, not a premise.\n\n### What the criticism actually shows, and to whom\n\nThe criticism shows something real and worth keeping: E009 overstates its case against WHL at the point where it says “every criticism is ultimately a criticism of a snapshot-level proposition,” because that sentence is not equivalent to P7’s per-target-independence claim, and the reader who reads E009 as a characterization of the P7 distinction will lose it. That is a defect in E009, and the criticism locates it precisely. But it is neither a defect in RSS (the criticism says so explicitly) nor a defect in the construct (the construct does not rely on the sentence in question).\n\n### What the criticism adds beyond restating the packet\n\nThe criticism adds a distinction the packet did not make explicit: **per-target independence of acceptance**, as opposed to **per-target indexing of a criticism**. These are not the same:\n\n- *Indexing*: a single criticism has a target slot, filled by an element from P7’s list. This is what the construct adds.\n- *Independence of acceptance*: an account may accept a rule while rejecting the record the rule is applied to, or reject the rule while accepting the record. This is what P7 claims matters, and what E009’s framing collapses.\n\nThe two are related but distinct. An account with a target-indexed criticism can still be committed proposition-globally if its commitment mechanism is proposition-global; conversely, an account can have independent per-target acceptances without having a target-indexed criticism vocabulary. RSS, read literally, admits both: accounts are constructed with *a set of accepted conjectures*, and nothing in RSS forces the accepted set to be proposition-globally consistent across targets, so per-target independence is available; the construct adds the indexing of a single criticism, which RSS does not perform as a primitive. The criticism therefore does add something beyond the packet.\n\nThat addition is worth keeping. It belongs in the construct’s surrounding notes, not inside RSS, and it does not change RSS or the construct.\n\n### Where the criticism over-reaches\n\nThe criticism’s transition from “E009’s sentence does not preserve P7’s distinction” to “the construct is in tension with the E009 occurrence it is packaged with” is valid only under the charitable reading being ruled out. Since the criticism itself leaves the charitable reading open, the transition is not established. Two further points:\n\n- The construct says “RSS already admits criticism of records, rules, coordinates, and assumptions as distinct elements.” This claims something about RSS, and RSS says it. It does not claim E009 said it, or that E009’s framing supports it.\n- The construct’s “target-conditional criticism” is not equivalent to E009’s “every criticism is ultimately a snapshot-level proposition.” The former indexes a criticism by its target; the latter claims there is only one target type. The construct does not inherit the latter.\n\n### What is preserved, and what is proposed\n\nPreserved unchanged from the frozen packet:\n\n- The frozen prose (P7, P9 and the rest of the corpus text) is untouched. Its claim that “one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late” remains as written.\n- RSS as the selected frozen carrier is untouched. Its primitive list — Story, Bag, Coordinate, Viewpoint, Conflict, Liveness, Reading, Projection — and its relations are unchanged. Its claim that “the criticism targets a specific element: a record, a rule, a booking history, a snapshot, a coordinate, or an assumption” is unchanged.\n- The E009 occurrence is untouched. Its proposed repairs (add `Account`, `Target`, `Assumption` primitives; filter duplicates; add `Choice`; swap bound names; declare `Quantity := Nat`; parameterize by deletion pre-filter; add a resource model) remain unsupported proposals, and remain in E009, not in RSS and not in the construct.\n- The construct (the “target-conditional reading of the compatibility objection”) is preserved as written. Its own notes already say, in slightly different words, what the criticism adds: “RSS already admits criticism of records, rules, coordinates, and assumptions as distinct elements.” I now add one neutral clarifying sentence to the construct’s notes, not to its body.\n\nProposed as a separate object, not installed anywhere:\n\n- An *acceptance-independence* note: that P7’s “reject a rule while accepting the data” is a claim about acceptance status varying per target within one account, distinct from the target-indexing of a single criticism. This is a proposal about how to read P7 alongside RSS; it is not claimed to be P7’s own claim, and it is not claimed to be required by RSS. It is offered so that a reader of the construct alongside E009 has the distinction available.\n\n### The successor disposition\n\nNo successor is warranted. The criticism establishes a defect in E009’s characterization of WHL, which the criticism itself leaves undecided (the charitable reading dissolves it). It does not establish a defect in RSS. It does not establish a defect in the construct, because the construct does not depend on E009’s sentence and does not attribute proposition-global acceptance to RSS. The criticism adds a distinction (acceptance-independence vs. indexing) that the frozen packet did not make explicit; that distinction is preserved here as an observation and as a separate proposal, not as an installed amendment to RFC, RSS, or the construct.\n\n### Re-issued construct, with a clarifying note added\n\nThe body of the construct is unchanged. The final “Notes on what is and is not claimed” gains one paragraph:\n\n> I am also distinguishing two things that can be confused in the presence of E009’s characterization of WHL. *Indexing a criticism by its target* is what this construct adds: a single criticism is read as a proposition indexed by an element of P7’s target list. *Acceptance-independence per target* is a separate claim that P7 makes and that RSS does not foreclose: an account may accept a rule while rejecting the data the rule is applied to, or reject the rule while accepting the data. These are related but not equivalent. RSS’s account vocabulary (a viewpoint, a reading, a set of accepted conjectures) does not force acceptance to be proposition-global, so per-target independence is available in RSS without any new primitive; the construct does not rely on that availability, but it also does not rule it out. E009’s sentence “every criticism is ultimately a criticism of a snapshot-level proposition” is a claim about WHL, and a strong reading of it would lose the P7 distinction; a charitable reading leaves the distinction intact. Whether the construct’s indirect expression of target-indexing (by constructing different accounts with different accepted conjectures) is equivalent to a dedicated target-indexing primitive remains, as noted above, unresolved.\n\n### What would matter next\n\nBeyond this packet, what would matter is: whether RSS’s construction of different accounts with different accepted conjectures can in fact distinguish “criticizing a coordinate” from “criticizing an assumption,” given that both are declared as targets but neither is given a distinct acceptance semantics in RSS. If they cannot be distinguished that way, the construct’s “target-conditional criticism” is doing less work than the construct suggests, and the corresponding claim about RSS’s existing vocabulary would need to be revised or replaced by a proposal to extend RSS’s account structure. That is the live residual question, and it is not resolved by the material at hand.", "commitments": "## Response to the criticism: no successor warranted; the construct is preserved\n\nThe criticism is a real reading of E009, and its central textual observation — that E009’s sentence “every criticism is ultimately a criticism of a snapshot-level proposition” is stronger than what P7 licenses — is correct as a reading of E009 in isolation. But the criticism does not establish a defect in the selected frozen RSS carrier, and it does not establish a defect in the new construct that would warrant a successor. I will explain why, then re-issue the construct unchanged with the relevant distinction made explicit.\n\n### Where the criticism lands and where it doesn’t\n\nThe criticism says three things:\n\n1. E009 conflates “types of thing criticized” with “types of conjecture,” and thereby loses P7’s claim that acceptance can vary per target independently.\n2. The new construct inherits E009’s framing where it says it “uses RSS’s existing account/commitment/refutation vocabulary” alongside E009.\n3. Therefore something about the construct (its “target-conditional criticism” claim) is in tension with the material it is packaged with.\n\nPoint 1 is correct about E009 read alone. The frozen P7 says the distinction matters because “one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late.” E009’s framing of WHL as having only snapshot-indexed conjectures is a claim about WHL, not about criticism in general, and the criticism’s charitable reading of that “So…” is the right one. But the criticism itself concedes this: “On that charitable reading, there is no defect in E009, only a compression of its scope.” That concession is decisive for the disposition. A reading which survives the strongest available reading of the text is not a defect.\n\nPoint 2 is the substantive move, and it is the one I reject. The construct says of itself:\n\n> I am not claiming that RSS needs a new primitive to represent target-conditional criticism. RSS already admits criticism of records, rules, coordinates, and assumptions as distinct elements; what I am adding is the indexing of a single criticism by its target, which RSS's current account structure does not explicitly perform but can express by constructing different accounts with different accepted conjectures.\n\nThat sentence does not depend on E009’s framing. It depends only on two RSS facts: that an account is (viewpoint, reading, accepted conjectures), and that a criticism targets a specific element among {record, rule, booking history, snapshot, coordinate, assumption}. Both facts are stated in RSS itself, not asserted by E009 about RSS. The construct attributes no propositional-global acceptance semantics to RSS, and RSS nowhere asserts one. The construct adds the *indexing of a single criticism by its target*, which RSS expresses indirectly by constructing different accounts with different accepted conjectures. Whether that indirect expression is equivalent to a dedicated indexing primitive is, as the construct says, unresolved.\n\nPoint 3 is therefore the weakest of the three. “Uses RSS’s existing vocabulary” is a literal description of the construct’s practice — the construct does not introduce any primitive not already in RSS. It does not say, and does not need to say, that E009’s characterization of WHL was correct. The construct does not depend on E009. E009 is a neighboring occurrence, not a premise.\n\n### What the criticism actually shows, and to whom\n\nThe criticism shows something real and worth keeping: E009 overstates its case against WHL at the point where it says “every criticism is ultimately a criticism of a snapshot-level proposition,” because that sentence is not equivalent to P7’s per-target-independence claim, and the reader who reads E009 as a characterization of the P7 distinction will lose it. That is a defect in E009, and the criticism locates it precisely. But it is neither a defect in RSS (the criticism says so explicitly) nor a defect in the construct (the construct does not rely on the sentence in question).\n\n### What the criticism adds beyond restating the packet\n\nThe criticism adds a distinction the packet did not make explicit: **per-target independence of acceptance**, as opposed to **per-target indexing of a criticism**. These are not the same:\n\n- *Indexing*: a single criticism has a target slot, filled by an element from P7’s list. This is what the construct adds.\n- *Independence of acceptance*: an account may accept a rule while rejecting the record the rule is applied to, or reject the rule while accepting the record. This is what P7 claims matters, and what E009’s framing collapses.\n\nThe two are related but distinct. An account with a target-indexed criticism can still be committed proposition-globally if