list — Story, Bag, Coordinate, Viewpoint, Conflict, Liveness, Reading, Projection — and its relations are unchanged. Its claim that “the criticism targets a specific element: a record, a rule, a booking history, a snapshot, a coordinate, or an assumption” is unchanged.\n- The E009 occurrence is untouched. Its proposed repairs (add `Account`, `Target`, `Assumption` primitives; filter duplicates; add `Choice`; swap bound names; declare `Quantity := Nat`; parameterize by deletion pre-filter; add a resource model) remain unsupported proposals, and remain in E009, not in RSS and not in the construct.\n- The construct (the “target-conditional reading of the compatibility objection”) is preserved as written. Its own notes already say, in slightly different words, what the criticism adds: “RSS already admits criticism of records, rules, coordinates, and assumptions as distinct elements.” I now add one neutral clarifying sentence to the construct’s notes, not to its body.\n\nProposed as a separate object, not installed anywhere:\n\n- An *acceptance-independence* note: that P7’s “reject a rule while accepting the data” is a claim about acceptance status varying per target within one account, distinct from the target-indexing of a single criticism. This is a proposal about how to read P7 alongside RSS; it is not claimed to be P7’s own claim, and it is not claimed to be required by RSS. It is offered so that a reader of the construct alongside E009 has the distinction available.\n\n### The successor disposition\n\nNo successor is warranted. The criticism establishes a defect in E009’s characterization of WHL, which the criticism itself leaves undecided (the charitable reading dissolves it). It does not establish a defect in RSS. It does not establish a defect in the construct, because the construct does not depend on E009’s sentence and does not attribute proposition-global acceptance to RSS. The criticism adds a distinction (acceptance-independence vs. indexing) that the frozen packet did not make explicit; that distinction is preserved here as an observation and as a separate proposal, not as an installed amendment to RFC, RSS, or the construct.\n\n### Re-issued construct, with a clarifying note added\n\nThe body of the construct is unchanged. The final “Notes on what is and is not claimed” gains one paragraph:\n\n> I am also distinguishing two things that can be confused in the presence of E009’s characterization of WHL. *Indexing a criticism by its target* is what this construct adds: a single criticism is read as a proposition indexed by an element of P7’s target list. *Acceptance-independence per target* is a separate claim that P7 makes and that RSS does not foreclose: an account may accept a rule while rejecting the data the rule is applied to, or reject the rule while accepting the data. These are related but not equivalent. RSS’s account vocabulary (a viewpoint, a reading, a set of accepted conjectures) does not force acceptance to be proposition-global, so per-target independence is available in RSS without any new primitive; the construct does not rely on that availability, but it also does not rule it out. E009’s sentence “every criticism is ultimately a criticism of a snapshot-level proposition” is a claim about WHL, and a strong reading of it would lose the P7 distinction; a charitable reading leaves the distinction intact. Whether the construct’s indirect expression of target-indexing (by constructing different accounts with different accepted conjectures) is equivalent to a dedicated target-indexing primitive remains, as noted above, unresolved.\n\n### What would matter next\n\nBeyond this packet, what would matter is: whether RSS’s construction of different accounts with different accepted conjectures can in fact distinguish “criticizing a coordinate” from “criticizing an assumption,” given that both are declared as targets but neither is given a distinct acceptance semantics in RSS. If they cannot be distinguished that way, the construct’s “target-conditional criticism” is doing less work than the construct suggests, and the corresponding claim about RSS’s existing vocabulary would need to be revised or replaced by a proposal to extend RSS’s account structure. That is the live residual question, and it is not resolved by the material at hand."}